Why is HRV important?
Your body is constantly responding to stress, from workouts to work deadlines to poor sleep. HRV is one window into how stressed or recovered your nervous system is.
Think of it this way: if your body is a phone battery, HRV tells you how much energy is left and how fast youre draining it. Low HRV might mean your battery is depleted. High HRV might mean youre charged and ready.
Why track it:
Know when your body genuinely requires rest rather than relying on guesswork
Make smarter training and recovery adjustments using objective data
Understand your nervous systems stress capacity without expensive lab test.
HRV wont tell you everything about your health. But its a useful clue about how your nervous system is managing stress and recovery.

Autonomic Nervous System and HRV
The autonomic nervous system regulates functions that largely occur without conscious control:
Heart rate
Blood pressure
Digestion
Breathing

Two branches are particularly relevant to understanding HRV:
A sympathetic nervous system helps your body respond to demands such as exercise, acute stress, and other challenges.
The parasympathetic nervous system supports resting and recovery functions. The vagus nerve plays an important role in cardiac regulation.

An important scientific clarification: HRV should not simply be interpreted as a measure of "sympathetic versus parasympathetic balance." HRV reflects interactions among several physiological regulatory systems, not just one simple balance.
Which HRV Metric Does Your Device Measure?
Different devices can report different HRV metrics. Understanding the measurement is important.
RMSSD (Root Mean Square of Successive Differences):
Reflects short-term beat-to-beat variation
Commonly used as an indicator of cardiac parasympathetic modulation
Frequently reported by consumer wearables
Can be useful for tracking changes in autonomic recovery

SDNN (Standard Deviation of Normal-to-Normal Intervals):
Describes overall variability during a recording period
Depends strongly on recording duration
A five-minute SDNN and a 24-hour SDNN should not be treated as interchangeable measurements

Frequency domain measures: HRV can also be analyzed according to frequency components, including high-frequency (HF) power, low-frequency (LF) power, and very-low-frequency (VLF) power.
